      "text": "To parameterize the line \\(y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}\\) in the form \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{v} + t \\mathbf{d}\\) such that the distance between \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(t\\) for \\(x \\ge 3\\), we need to determine the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) and the initial point \\(\\mathbf{v}\\).\n\nFirst, let's identify \\(\\mathbf{v}\\). The point \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) lies on the line. Substituting \\(x = 3\\) into the line equation \\(y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}\\), we get:\n\\[ y = \\frac{3(3) - 5}{4} = \\frac{9 - 5}{4} = 1. \\]\nSo, the point \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is on the line, and we can take \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) must be parallel to the line \\(y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}\\). The slope of the line is \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\), so we can parameterize the direction vector as \\(\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This is because the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) has a slope of \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\), which is the same as the slope of the line.\n\nNow, we have the parameterization:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nTo verify, we substitute \\(x = 3 + 4t\\) and \\(y = 1 + 3t\\) into the line equation \\(y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}\\):\n\\[\n1 + 3t = \\frac{3(3 + 4t) - 5}{4} = \\frac{9 + 12t - 5}{4} = \\frac{4 + 12t}{4} = 1 + 3t.\n\\]\nThis confirms that the parameterization is correct. Therefore, the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
