      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The given line equation is in point-slope form. To parameterize the line in the form \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{v} + t \\mathbf{d}\\), we need to identify the point \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) and the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\).\n\nFirst, we find the point \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) by setting \\(y = 1\\) (since \\(y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}\\) and we want to find the point where \\(y = 1\\)):\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{3x - 5}{4} \\implies 4 = 3x - 5 \\implies 3x = 9 \\implies x = 3.\n\\]\nSo, the point \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNext, we find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). The slope of the line is given by the coefficient of \\(x\\) in the numerator over the coefficient of \\(x\\) in the denominator, which is \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\). Therefore, the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) can be written as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (we can scale this vector to match the slope).\n\nSo, the parameterization of the line is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the distance between \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(t\\). To verify this, we calculate the distance between the two points:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2}.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(x = 3 + 4t\\) and \\(y = 1 + 3t\\) into the distance formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(3 + 4t - 3)^2 + (1 + 3t - 1)^2} = \\sqrt{(4t)^2 + (3t)^2} = \\sqrt{16t^2 + 9t^2} = \\sqrt{25t^2} = 5t.\n\\]\nThis confirms that the distance between the point on the line and the point \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is indeed \\(t\\).\n\nTherefore, the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
